# XCU renice
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Each test starts a sleeping child of its own and reads its nice value with
# ps -o nice. Only raising the nice value is done, which needs no privilege.
# -g and -u act on whole groups or users and would touch processes of the
# harness, so they are not exercised.

assertion  XCU.renice.001
anchor     utilities/renice.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/renice.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       renice -n 1 PID, with the process ID given as an operand and no
           other option, exits with status zero and raises the nice value of
           that process by one, or leaves it at the upper limit when it was
           there already.
test       t/001-increment.sh

assertion  XCU.renice.002
anchor     utilities/renice.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       renice -n 2 -p PID acts on the process with that ID in the same way
           as the form without -p: status zero and a nice value two higher or
           at the upper limit.
test       t/002-p-option.sh

assertion  XCU.renice.003
anchor     utilities/renice.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       An increment that would go beyond the implementation's upper limit
           for the nice value is not an error: renice -n 1000 PID exits with
           status zero and the nice value does not go down.
test       t/003-bounded-increment.sh

assertion  XCU.renice.004
anchor     utilities/renice.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/renice.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       renice of a process ID that does not exist exits with a status
           above zero.
test       t/004-no-such-process.sh

assertion  XCU.renice.005
anchor     utilities/renice.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/renice.html#STDERR
class      required
says       A successful renice writes nothing to standard output.
test       t/005-no-stdout.sh

assertion  XCU.renice.006
anchor     utilities/renice.html#OPTIONS
class      untestable
says       -g applies the change to every process of a process group and -u
           to every process of a user, by user name or numeric ID.
reason     Both change the nice value of processes the test does not own, in
           the harness's own group or user; a safe group or user cannot be
           arranged portably from a script.
